data.content:
# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: BackgroundProcessor

## Critical Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 6+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`**
3. **`ThreadAbortException` is never raised** by the runtime

The entire cancellation model must be replaced with **`CancellationToken`** cooperative cancellation.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(token), token);
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass the token so blocking calls can be interrupted
                var item = GetNextItem(token);
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop cleanly
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Task didn't finish in time — log it; you cannot force-kill it
            Log.Warning("Worker did not stop within timeout");
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Blocking calls ignore cancellation** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a `Monitor`, `Socket`, or `Console.ReadLine`, cancellation won't interrupt it. You must pass `CancellationToken` into blocking APIs or switch to async variants (`await ReadAsync(token)`). |
| **No force-kill equivalent** | `Thread.Abort()` had no safe replacement — there is **no way** to forcibly terminate a thread in .NET 8. All cancellation must be cooperative. |
| **`catch (ThreadAbortException)`** | This catch block will **never execute** in .NET 8. If you depend on it for rollback logic, move that to `catch (OperationCanceledException)`. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | A volatile flag can't wake a blocked thread. `CancellationToken` can — via `token.Register()` or by passing it to blocking APIs like `BlockingCollection.Take(token)`. |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Unlike background threads, unobserved `Task` exceptions are swallowed. Always `await` the task in `StopAsync` or attach a continuation to log faults. |

## If `GetNextItem` Uses a BlockingCollection

This is the most common real-world pattern:

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework)
var item = _queue.Take(); // blocks forever, only Thread.Abort stops it

// After (.NET 8)
var item = _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
```

## If You Need an Async Version

For new code, prefer `IHostedService` / `BackgroundService`:

```csharp
public class MyProcessor : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            var item = await GetNextItemAsync(stoppingToken);
            await ProcessItemAsync(item, stoppingToken);
        }
    }
}
```

This integrates with the .NET hosting model and handles lifetime management automatically via `Host.RunAsync()`.
